comments.body:
@participant1 I have seen a lot of changes by you in libnetwork in the recent weeks. I'd be interested in your gut feeling here. Would this be a bad idea?

If this is something that is worth exploring I'd be happy to help with the implementation. This has been bugging us for a lot of time and if this helps the way that I envision it, this would make our clusters so much more reliable.

/cc @neersighted

@participant2 Did you get the chance to look into this? I am somewhat out of answers at this point with our clusters.

This materializes heavily during upgrades of the cluster. How can we workaround this? Should we just not restart the nodes in quick succession?

How can I help debugging this?

@reporter @participant4 Did you guys come up with a workaround yet? Did Flannel work for either of you? I've had this happen to me couple of days back and it's a major blocker to move Docker Swarm to production.

The only easy "solution" I can think of is to reduce chances of it happening by staggering deploys and making sure nodes don't update at the same time. And needs reboot of nodes when it does happen.

@participant2 Not that I can complain while using an open source product and not having means to contribute anything back, but this is a pretty huge bug IMO. Are there plans to take a deeper look at this from Mirantis?

@participant5 I'm in the final stages of an implementation that completely replaces swarms overlay network and internal DNS server.
I don't know if it's the right place to talk about it but look at my companies GitHub and it should be obvious 

@participant2 if it helps, I actually was able to create a stable reproduction of some of these issues. I'll put them in a repo in the coming days and link it here.
